  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1028 PM MDT Fri May 15 2026\n\nLittle to no change in the forecast from previous forecast packages \nwith warm temperatures and breezy winds through the weekend and into \nearly next week.\n\n...SATURDAY & SUNDAY...\n\nBroad, longwave trough across western CONUS will overlay increasing \nsouthwesterly flow aloft starting on Saturday. The first lobe of \nenergy associated with the longwave trough will race across the \nGreat Basin and Four Corners regions on Saturday. In response to \nthat, 993mb surface low will develop over SE Colorado with \nsufficient surface troughing across New Mexico. Winds will be breezy \nduring the afternoon at 15-25 mph with gusts up to 35 mph across the \narea. Temperatures on Saturday will be in the lower 90s across \nthe desert lowlands.\n\nOn Sunday, strong southwest flow aloft will overlay the region. Sub-\n990mb surface low will once again develop over SE Colorado with \nstrong surface troughing across New Mexico. The tightly packed \nsurface pressure gradient and upper level jet streak overhead will \npromote windy conditions across the area. Winds will be out of the \nsouthwest at 20-30 mph sustained with gusts to 40-45 mph possible.\n\n...MONDAY & TUESDAY...\n\nThe aforementioned upper level trough will finally trek across the \nGreat Basin and Central Rockies on Monday. SWerly flow aloft will \ncontinue overlaying the region with surface troughing across New \nMexico. Winds will be breezy once again at 15-25 mph sustained with \ngusts to 35 mph possible. Breezy winds continue on Tuesday with \ntemperatures dropping below 90 degrees.\n\n...REMAINDER OF THE WORK WEEK...\n\nUpper level trough moves east of the region midweek. As it does, \nmodels all agree on a weak low/trough setting up off the Baja coast. \nAs it does, winds will shift around to the southeast and push the \ndryline toward the RGV. Always some question with the initial \nwestward push with these, but there is decent agreement there will \nbe some storm chances at least in Hudspeth and Otero counties. \nDoesn't look like much cooling except out in the far east.",
